Expected Outcomes
A working Jenkins pipeline (failure-handling-pipeline) that demonstrates both the built-in retry() step and a custom exponential-backoff retry loop, with email notifications configured for success and failure.
A second pipeline (advanced-error-handling-pipeline) using reusable Groovy helper functions (retryWithBackoff, handleStageFailure, sendNotification, generateBuildReport) and a deployWithRollback function that performs an HTTP health check and automatically rolls back a failed deployment.
An archived build-report.txt artifact summarizing each build's result and any failed stages, usable for auditing pipeline reliability over time.
Troubleshooting
Symptom: emailext step throws an error such as ERROR: Could not connect to SMTP host. Diagnostic hint: Check Manage Jenkins > System > Extended E-mail Notification for correct SMTP host/port/credentials; in an isolated lab without real mail access, this failure is expected and does not invalidate the pipeline logic â confirm instead that the post block was entered by checking the console output text just before the error.
Symptom: The Deploy stage's health check always fails, even though serve.py appears to start. Diagnostic hint: Run cat serve.log in the workspace and check whether port 8099 is already in use from a previous build (pkill -f serve.py should have cleaned it up) or whether curl is installed on the agent (which curl).
Conclusion
In this lab, you built progressively more sophisticated Jenkins pipelines for handling failures: starting with the built-in retry() step, moving to a manual exponential-backoff loop, and finally assembling a full set of reusable Groovy helper functions that track failed stages, send status notifications, and generate an auditable build report. You also implemented a realistic deploy-with-rollback pattern, where a deployed service is validated with an HTTP health check and automatically rolled back if it fails to respond correctly. These patternsâretries with backoff, centralized failure tracking, proactive notifications, and health-check-driven rollbackâare foundational techniques for building CI/CD pipelines that fail safely and recover gracefully rather than leaving systems in a broken state. As a next step, consider extending the health check to validate application-specific behavior beyond a simple status code, integrating a real messaging platform for notifications, and exploring Jenkins shared libraries so these helper functions can be reused across many pipelines instead of being copied into each Jenkinsfile.
